In a #medium saucepan{}, combine @milk{2%cups} and @heavy cream{1%cup} and turn on the heat to medium low.

> Generally, you can play loose with the ratios here, so long as you have mostly milk to heavy cream. You could also do 3 cups half-and-half or use a light cream.

Add @sugar{3/4%cup} and @salt{1/8%tsp} to the milk mixture and stir together. Heat until sugar/salt are dissolved, then remove from heat. Remove the saucepan from heat if you see small bubbles around the edges of the pan. You should not let the mixture boil.

Place @matcha powder{5%tbsp}(1 tbsp = 6g) in a #medium bowl{}. Add 3-4 tbsp of the heated milk mixture. Stir with a silicone spatula until the matcha completely absorbs the liquid and no lumps remain.

Add another 3-4 tbsp milk mixture and stir until the matcha absorbs the liquid.

For a third time, add another 3-4 tbsp of the milk mixture and stir. The mixture will start to become pasty. Stir and mash the paste to incorporate any dry lumps of powder.

For the fourth time, add another 3-4 tbsp of milk mixture and stir to further loosen up any paste. Continue working out lumps as you go.

Add a final 3-4 tbsp of the milk mixture to the bowl and stir. Now the mixture should be a thick liquid with no lumps. If it is not yet this texture, add more small additions of milk mixture until it is the right consistency.

Transfer the matcha mixture into the milk mixture and stir together.

Change to a whisk and combine this matcha ice cream mixture well.

Prepare an ice bath and bowl that can sit in the ice bath. Set a fine-mesh sieve or cheesecloth over the bowl. Pour the mixture through the sieve/cloth

Press and strain any matcha lumps left. Let the mixture cool. Once the mixture has cooled, cover with plastic wrap and chill in the refrigerator for at least 4 hours. This is important to ensure ice cream-like consistency.

When ready to make ice cream, whisk the ice cream mixture one last time. Then transfer to the maker bowl and churn according to manufacturer's instruction

After about 25 minutes, the mixture should be thickened and no longer moving. Transfer the ice cream to an air tight container. Freeze for at least 4 hours.